00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.220
PROFESSOR: Today is pretty
much just set aside for

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.080
brainstorming.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.202
So we're probably going to
do about an hour of that.

00:00:26.202 --> 00:00:28.396
Just to throw the ideas that
people already have in their

00:00:28.396 --> 00:00:32.246
heads about what could be with
the problem space that we're

00:00:32.246 --> 00:00:34.442
working on.

00:00:34.442 --> 00:00:41.381
This whole preparing people to
deal with violence and folks

00:00:41.381 --> 00:00:42.840
suffering from an illness.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.922
And there's a whole bunch of
different things that people

00:00:45.922 --> 00:00:47.070
may be working on.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.150
And the idea is that hopefully
we can get this all out and

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:53.020
then we can spend the next
half-hour, and frankly the

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.920
rest of this week, trying
to figure out

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.000
who's in which team.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.002
Who's collaborating on a
similar topic and who's

00:01:00.002 --> 00:01:03.580
interested in what.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.040
And then, anyone actually get
through any of the readings?

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:07.760
OK, great.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.160
So there's a few things
up there.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.926
I realize that we don't have
a lot of time to read.

00:01:11.926 --> 00:01:14.550
And there's probably more stuff
up there to read that

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.550
needs to be read necessarily.

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.770
But it's all there
as resources.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:21.555
Any ideas that people
thought might be

00:01:21.555 --> 00:01:22.805
interesting to work on?

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.220
Andrew?

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.560
AUDIENCE: So one of the basic
issues I heard from the

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.220
presentation, and the papers,
was communication.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:35.330
So this may work as a
simulation, but the real world

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:43.055
system would be communication
between the psychiatrist and

00:01:43.055 --> 00:01:44.050
the patient.

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:47.990
Along with the psychiatrist with
the person above them.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.830
PROFESSOR: Psychiatrist,
patient, communication?

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.080
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:01:53.435 --> 00:01:55.890
What do they call them?

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.250
Not the patient.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.130
Patient, psychiatrist?

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.840
AUDIENCE: Orderly?

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:01.285
AUDIENCE: Nurse?

00:02:01.285 --> 00:02:03.070
AUDIENCE: What function
do they have?

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.320
AUDIENCE: Give us more
information.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.820
AUDIENCE: Psychiatrist-Patient
for now that's fine.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.650
PROFESSOR: You know how talking
about psychiatrists

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.055
there is patient.

00:02:14.055 --> 00:02:16.180
Psychiatrist, resident?

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:17.920
AUDIENCE: Maybe like nurse,
patient, and then nurse

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.420
reporting incidents
to supervisors?

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:22.920
PROFESSOR:Three levels?

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.002
AUDIENCE: Yes, that's the one.

00:02:25.002 --> 00:02:27.210
PROFESSOR: So it would go
supervisor, nurse--

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.850
AUDIENCE: I'm sorry, guys.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:29.806
PROFESSOR: --and you
have the patient.

00:02:29.806 --> 00:02:32.196
Those kinds of reporting
up and reporting down.

00:02:32.196 --> 00:02:35.015
All right, yeah?

00:02:35.015 --> 00:02:36.420
AUDIENCE: So I had
a couple ideas.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:41.040
One of them was, every idea you
have a patient and there's

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.900
two doctors or psychiatrists.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:49.350
And so one psychiatrist is
trying to calm the patient

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.530
while the other is doing the
opposite and trying to make

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.620
this patient as angry
as possible.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:58.280
So the way I was thinking was,
the psychiatrist that's trying

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.290
to enrage the patient?

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.010
That's training in
what not to say.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.720
While the doctor who is trying
to calm them down is--

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:12.820
PROFESSOR: So by showing what's
the worst possible way?

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.476
OK, I'm going to call that
good cop, bad cop.

00:03:15.476 --> 00:03:18.030
AUDIENCE: --Yeah, and then
another idea I had which I

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.990
thought of with [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
yesterday was

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:26.390
have a team of psychiatrists
working with one patient.

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:31.860
But interlaced in one of the
psychiatrists is a saboteur

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.820
trying to enrage the
patient as well.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:39.594
PROFESSOR: And what are the
target audiences going to be

00:03:39.594 --> 00:03:40.844
like actual residents.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:48.080
I'm just going to say a
saboteur in a group of

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.330
psychiatrists.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.277
Because maybe there's an
idea under that dynamic

00:03:55.277 --> 00:03:56.470
that we can play with.

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:57.620
OK?

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.340
AUDIENCE: OK, so a
few quick things.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.290
One would be, I'm not
exactly sure how

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:03.980
this fits into a game.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.690
But simulating people who are
delusional schizophrenic by

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:12.700
either having every player have
a separate audio track

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:14.250
playing in their ear
as they play?

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.339
So there's voices speaking to
them and they have different

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:17.140
audio experiences.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:21.350
Or having just one speaker that
everyone can hear that's

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:22.175
playing audio.

00:04:22.175 --> 00:04:24.250
So incorporating audio into the
experience to simulate in

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.640
some way the internal thought
processes and perhaps these

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:27.560
delusional thoughts.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:30.308
The voice that tells you to go
kill your mother and what not.

00:04:30.308 --> 00:04:32.220
Which is a documented issue.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.663
PROFESSOR: This particular
game doesn't have to be a

00:04:33.663 --> 00:04:35.330
board game, doesn't have to be
a card game, you have a lot

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.830
more flexibility in what
you want to do.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:40.137
I would suggest steering away
from doing anything that

00:04:40.137 --> 00:04:41.270
requires digital logic.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:44.966
But MP3 players would
be perfect.

00:04:44.966 --> 00:04:46.820
AUDIENCE: Exactly, something
like that.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:49.205
So the second thing would be a
board game where you're in

00:04:49.205 --> 00:04:50.170
charge of managing a ward.

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:51.280
It's just a logistical game.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.690
Making sure that you don't
leave anyone neglected or

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.445
forget anyone.

00:04:57.445 --> 00:05:01.860
So your patients have their
favorite nurse, and they have

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.500
issues that you have to
uncover and deal with.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.670
And if you leave somebody
unattended and someone goes

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:07.510
over they may become violent.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.700
So you've got to try
and make sure.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:13.483
And then the third idea would be
to have one player play as

00:05:13.483 --> 00:05:16.620
the patient and to simulate
their inability to communicate

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.420
properly for whatever reason.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.803
The information of their
condition is

00:05:20.803 --> 00:05:21.970
hidden from the player.

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:25.510
And so try to create a game
experience that is genuinely

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:29.940
frustrating because the player
doesn't quite know what's

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.530
wrong with them and can't
articulate it properly.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.880
And the other players are in
charge of finding it out.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:35.990
And putting some kind of
time-constraint things where

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.020
it gets heated.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:38.653
PROFESSOR: There are a bunch
of improv games that are

00:05:38.653 --> 00:05:39.380
exactly that.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.230
I have something about me but
can't tell you what it is.

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:46.150
AUDIENCE: But the players know
that it can get really, yeah.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:47.872
AUDIENCE: That's like the game
where you have, I can't

00:05:47.872 --> 00:05:50.530
remember what it's called, but
the one where you have to try

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:52.350
to make the other person
say a word.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.866
But you can't use a certain
series of key words.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.700
AUDIENCE: I'm simulating
schizophrenia with audio tape.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.850
Being part of RPG's which
have done that well.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.270
So like live-action role
playing stuff.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:12.530
Which did it really well.

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:13.510
It actually works.

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:17.350
So I recommend that.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:20.561
It's been tried in a setting
which I've seen before and

00:06:20.561 --> 00:06:21.050
worked really well.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:23.770
So I think it could work out.

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:28.000
PROFESSOR: You could do a
refinement on that idea.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.770
AUDIENCE: So I know one of the
main things that he was

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:34.080
talking about was knowing what
to do and just going through

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.530
the process of what happens when
somebody does something.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:39.990
And so that brought to mind
D & D, and how you have to

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.840
prepare for the encounter and
then in the encounter you have

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.960
certain actions that
you can do.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.290
Calm through speech, calm
through presenting some

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:50.780
object, or some idea
or whatever.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:55.210
Which I think would map pretty
easily into the D & D space.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:58.550
And then you could also have
certain abilities which are

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:01.240
natural to your character like
you're very charismatic which

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:04.790
makes some things
easier and such.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:11.542
So simplify D & D to handle
interactions with patients.

00:07:11.542 --> 00:07:14.080
PROFESSOR: So table-top
RPG style.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.120
And the kind of things you want
to emphasize are things

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:20.950
like preparation and playing
up your actual skills?

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:21.742
AUDIENCE: Right.

00:07:21.742 --> 00:07:23.218
PROFESSOR: Things that
you're good at.

00:07:27.646 --> 00:07:28.896
So natural skills
and preparation.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:36.610
AUDIENCE: So I had an idea for
a game that could potentially

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:37.550
be a card game.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:40.520
Where the player who's playing
as the patient and the player

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.310
who's playing as the doctor
are operating

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:43.750
under different rules.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:46.240
And one of the main points of
the game is they're not

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:48.150
actually aware of the other
player's rules.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:50.450
So, for instance the patient
could have certain goals which

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:52.000
he is trying to achieve.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.020
But he is unaware which of
these goals is completely

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:56.780
unreasonable to the doctor.

00:07:56.780 --> 00:08:01.220
And the doctor has certain
actions he can take and is

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:02.530
unaware which of these
are going to be

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.150
threatening to the patient.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.970
And so, then there could be an
instance where the patient

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:09.330
actually believes the only way
he is capable of winning the

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.000
game is by using violence.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:14.184
And so that could be one
of the end conditions.

00:08:14.184 --> 00:08:15.627
PROFESSOR: So, sure.

00:08:15.627 --> 00:08:19.480
And you have to deduce
that out?

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:21.020
Moving on to Michelle?

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:23.890
AUDIENCE: I like the idea that
a psychiatrist has to really

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:26.170
keep their composure when
dealing with a violent person.

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:27.050
They can't raise their voice.

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:29.200
They have to keep at
a certain distance.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.740
And they can't seem upset
for whatever reason.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:37.230
Sort of a party game idea, you
could have two people.

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:39.570
Basically, one person would
just have to be throwing

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:40.576
insults at the other person.

00:08:40.576 --> 00:08:42.659
And the person who's receiving
the insults would just have to

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:45.800
stay calm the whole time and
continue to talking.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:47.370
Which is really hard to do even
if you know it's coming.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:49.320
PROFESSOR: So keep your cool.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:52.050
I mean, that could be a game
where you're literally

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:54.210
insulting someone or it could
be a thing where you're

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.630
playing a character with a
certain amount of composure

00:08:56.630 --> 00:09:01.045
points or something and you're
planning actions and

00:09:01.045 --> 00:09:03.812
maintaining notes.

00:09:03.812 --> 00:09:06.800
And different tactics to be
able to increase those or

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:09.650
decrease those.

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:12.610
AUDIENCE: A co-op type of game
where you are a bunch of

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.830
nurses trying to take
care of the ward.

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:17.950
This is a lot like Jeremy's
Diner Dash style game.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:20.370
Except, you have a limitation
to how you can

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:21.190
actually do it once.

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:23.865
So you will be forced to
neglect one often.

00:09:23.865 --> 00:09:24.150
PROFESSOR: A patient, one?

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:25.400
Neglect one patient?

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:29.900
AUDIENCE: So nurses
will get sick and

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:31.150
they'll have to leave.

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:37.455
PROFESSOR: So, rather you
have limited resources.

00:09:37.455 --> 00:09:40.365
Not quite nearly enough to deal
with every single crisis

00:09:40.365 --> 00:09:42.800
that's going to happen.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.570
AUDIENCE: So the one thing I
found interesting in reading

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:49.580
this stuff is statistically the
amount of violence that

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:54.996
happens with mental patients is
not actually that different

00:09:54.996 --> 00:09:56.324
from the amount of violence that
happens in the general

00:09:56.324 --> 00:09:57.190
population.

00:09:57.190 --> 00:10:00.220
And it talks about how people
going through this, and

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:02.210
deciding to go into the
profession, might actually

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:03.640
second guess themselves
because they have this

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:05.095
perception of there's
more violence.

00:10:05.095 --> 00:10:08.365
So I think incorporating
something where you have life

00:10:08.365 --> 00:10:13.050
in the ward and life
outside of it.

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:15.670
So you incorporate both the
things where you're trying to

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:18.700
deal with patients but you're
also reinforcing this idea

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:23.200
that it's not significantly
more dangerous

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.680
than real life is.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:26.160
AUDIENCE: None of your patients
stab you but you get

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:26.610
mugged on the way home.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.280
AUDIENCE: Yeah, something
like that.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:31.140
It's just life.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:39.950
PROFESSOR: One thing that could
be really attractive is

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:43.060
that now broadens your audience
to people who aren't

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.670
just psychiatric residents.

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.230
AUDIENCE: So, I like the idea
of the patient and doctor

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:55.590
having different roles, rules,
goals, views, et cetera.

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:59.740
But I also like the idea of
incorporating an escalation

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:02.330
and de-escalation into
the conflict

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:03.850
between the two of them.

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:07.160
So I feel like part of the
thing which was really

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:08.280
compelling about the
role-playing scenario which

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:12.250
they showed was there was then
sense in which the doctor can

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:17.054
say things which can escalate or
de-escalate the situation.

00:11:17.054 --> 00:11:20.750
Make it more or less likely that
things will go a bad way.

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:24.577
And one way which that could
be done partially through

00:11:24.577 --> 00:11:29.620
roles would be to have hidden
keywords which the patient

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:32.468
responds to or what have you
and that moves them into a

00:11:32.468 --> 00:11:32.800
different state.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:36.726
And then transitioning between
these two states changes the

00:11:36.726 --> 00:11:41.780
goals which the patient has so
maybe the intent on trying to

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:43.480
get Nurse-- what was
the nurse's name?

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:43.810
Dave?

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:44.210
AUDIENCE: John.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.000
AUDIENCE: Nurse John.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:48.800
But as they get de-escalated
then they just want to change

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:49.210
their meds.

00:11:49.210 --> 00:11:54.410
But then as they get
de-escalated from that then

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:56.010
they just want to
go run back out.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:12:00.139
PROFESSOR: Just coming up with
the rules that model what an

00:12:00.139 --> 00:12:05.290
unreasonable patient would
do or what are you

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:06.500
willing to give up.

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:10.144
And actually putting somebody
in the shoes of that, is

00:12:10.144 --> 00:12:11.675
essentially something
to think about what

00:12:11.675 --> 00:12:14.710
I can do as a doctor.

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:16.542
AUDIENCE: So, there was this
guy we used to play in my

00:12:16.542 --> 00:12:19.700
family where you're given a
scenario and certain number of

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:23.830
words you can't use.

