00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:36.640
PROFESSOR: So two people playing
one character at once.

00:26:36.640 --> 00:26:39.140
AUDIENCE: It would potentially
require a lot of suspension of

00:26:39.140 --> 00:26:41.500
disbelief on the part of
the person playing the

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:41.870
psychiatrist.

00:26:41.870 --> 00:26:44.940
PROFESSOR: It might be one of
those things that's easier to

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:50.336
mediate by having people
talk on screen or

00:26:50.336 --> 00:26:53.312
something like that?

00:26:53.312 --> 00:26:55.296
Or a microphone in a
speaker type thing?

00:26:55.296 --> 00:26:56.546
[INAUDIBLE PHRASE]

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:01.496
I've not seen many games that
are designed specifically to

00:27:01.496 --> 00:27:03.460
be played on the [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:05.960
function.

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:07.466
AUDIENCE: I definitely like
that idea, and I think it

00:27:07.466 --> 00:27:08.760
could work well in a
card game as well.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:11.340
If you have two players sharing
the same hand, and the

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:13.950
player who actually gets to play
the cards from the hand

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:16.610
is whoever's in control
of the patient.

00:27:16.610 --> 00:27:21.580
And so the state basically
changes depending on what the

00:27:21.580 --> 00:27:22.690
doctor does.

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:26.110
Different players come in
control of the patient's hand.

00:27:26.110 --> 00:27:28.300
PROFESSOR: So it seems like
we actually have a lot of

00:27:28.300 --> 00:27:32.740
different options on how we
could mechanic someone who's

00:27:32.740 --> 00:27:33.990
going to be unstable.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:39.237
And also, a bunch of ideas on
how that state switch could

00:27:39.237 --> 00:27:42.950
happen or what makes
it take over.

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:44.270
So that's pretty cool.

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:49.170
And that's not mutually
exclusive from any other ideas

00:27:49.170 --> 00:27:49.930
that are specifically
about the patient

00:27:49.930 --> 00:27:51.430
and the doctor, right?

00:27:51.430 --> 00:27:55.570
So we're talking about what
happens to the patient.

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:57.610
These are the rules a patient
could be operating under.

00:27:57.610 --> 00:27:59.176
The doctor could have a
different set of rules.

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:05.286
AUDIENCE: I was reading about
this, and I think that it

00:28:05.286 --> 00:28:09.970
turns out most violence in
psychiatric wards happens to

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:13.130
the patients themselves or to
other patients by patients.

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:18.270
Much more than it actually
happens towards practitioners.

00:28:18.270 --> 00:28:21.780
And I'm just noticing that I
don't think any of our ideas

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:24.560
address any of those issues.

00:28:24.560 --> 00:28:28.570
PROFESSOR: Ward management maybe
gets a little closer.

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:31.410
AUDIENCE: People start turning
on themselves.

00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:34.160
And I was just thinking another
possible way you could

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:37.230
apply a lot of these
role-playing multiple role

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:40.310
auditory mechanics is
to a situation.

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:44.490
We should probably do research
in what situations lead to

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:48.025
patients coming in contact
with each other and the

00:28:48.025 --> 00:28:49.980
encounter turning violent.

00:28:49.980 --> 00:28:51.940
And all players be patients that
have various different

00:28:51.940 --> 00:28:56.140
mental disorders, and just to
help you understand as a

00:28:56.140 --> 00:28:58.840
patient what kinds of behaviors
and situations would

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:03.450
lead you to going violent
on someone else.

00:29:03.450 --> 00:29:05.870
Just like one of those, be in
someone else's shoes helps you

00:29:05.870 --> 00:29:07.300
understand the situation
better.

00:29:07.300 --> 00:29:12.520
PROFESSOR: So we're just putting
them in an environment

00:29:12.520 --> 00:29:16.857
where everything that is
necessary for a different

00:29:16.857 --> 00:29:21.828
character to become upset and
violent and just see how it

00:29:21.828 --> 00:29:22.784
naturally occurs.

00:29:22.784 --> 00:29:24.034
[INAUDIBLE].

00:29:26.620 --> 00:29:27.990
OK.

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:30.500
AUDIENCE: They gave a persuasion
that's a cross

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:32.500
between BS and Uno.

00:29:32.500 --> 00:29:34.280
So you have two doctors who are
trying to get rid of cards

00:29:34.280 --> 00:29:38.560
in their hand, and the patient
wants a certain set of cards.

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:40.985
And he's saying, I'm
looking for John.

00:29:40.985 --> 00:29:45.182
And the two doctors pull out a
hard and say, oh this is John.

00:29:45.182 --> 00:29:46.829
Or they can be like, oh this
isn't John but I can

00:29:46.829 --> 00:29:48.079
help you out later.

00:29:50.520 --> 00:29:54.496
PROFESSOR: That almost seems
like it's intended to teach

00:29:54.496 --> 00:29:55.490
people about lying.

00:29:55.490 --> 00:29:56.981
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:29:56.981 --> 00:30:02.448
PROFESSOR: So, I'm going
to say it's almost

00:30:02.448 --> 00:30:04.436
like BS and Go Fish.

00:30:17.358 --> 00:30:20.360
So we have a pretty
good pile here.

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:25.860
A lot of these can obviously
be combined.

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:28.890
And there's no reason why
multiple groups couldn't

00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:30.940
attempt to do similar topics.

00:30:30.940 --> 00:30:34.295
In fact, you could take exactly
the same set and I'm

00:30:34.295 --> 00:30:36.760
pretty sure you're going
to [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:38.346
So that's fine.

00:30:38.346 --> 00:30:42.420
Again, a lot of things that
could probably be easier done

00:30:42.420 --> 00:30:43.200
live-action.

00:30:43.200 --> 00:30:46.280
Or easier to do a prototype
live-action.

00:30:46.280 --> 00:30:53.020
The folks who were here on
Monday, they are actually

00:30:53.020 --> 00:30:58.912
focused mostly on trying to see
what could be moved out to

00:30:58.912 --> 00:31:03.180
more resonant things
in the future.

00:31:03.180 --> 00:31:04.785
Your games don't necessarily
need to do that.

00:31:04.785 --> 00:31:08.910
But you might want to
keep that in mind.

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:12.834
Because this assignment is a
game for a client after all.

00:31:12.834 --> 00:31:15.750
And if you manage to
address that need--

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:18.880
here's something that doesn't
require one person from the

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:20.477
original GM team.

00:31:24.149 --> 00:31:28.620
You can imagine the acting
exercise that we ran through,

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:30.930
beyond talented improvising
on their feet.

00:31:35.280 --> 00:31:40.456
Requires people and requires
responses that may not

00:31:40.456 --> 00:31:44.963
necessarily every gaming
environment's going to have.

00:31:44.963 --> 00:31:48.330
Something that can be turned
into a very understandable set

00:31:48.330 --> 00:31:52.974
of rules or using technologies
that are very accessible.

00:31:52.974 --> 00:31:56.132
They're going to play your voice
and its going to be a

00:31:56.132 --> 00:32:00.102
lot easier than something like
formulas or something like a

00:32:00.102 --> 00:32:04.638
phone as opposed
to [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:04.638 --> 00:32:07.730
AUDIENCE: It was a phone
conversation actually going?

00:32:07.730 --> 00:32:10.244
So if it's live, and people are
in different rooms, and

00:32:10.244 --> 00:32:12.584
actually talking to each other
over the phone or Skype or

00:32:12.584 --> 00:32:13.172
something like that.

00:32:13.172 --> 00:32:14.980
But you're talking to someone?

00:32:14.980 --> 00:32:17.159
You have the phone-ups and then
that other person can

00:32:17.159 --> 00:32:19.270
hear what you're saying
and be the evil voice?

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:23.229
PROFESSOR: That's the thing,
you don't even

00:32:23.229 --> 00:32:23.850
need to do it on Skype.

00:32:23.850 --> 00:32:26.830
You just get two cell phones
with [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:32:29.520 --> 00:32:33.140
Now, the technology just becomes
that much easier for

00:32:33.140 --> 00:32:34.390
games [INAUDIBLE PHRASE].

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:43.320
AUDIENCE: Or you could make it
like if there's some game that

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:44.100
two people are playing.

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:46.120
The two groups that are playing
the same game but they

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:47.530
both hear the other group.

00:32:47.530 --> 00:32:49.230
Like this group would
hear the other

00:32:49.230 --> 00:32:50.970
group's playing the game.

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:52.725
So it's not even just a
random conversation.

00:32:52.725 --> 00:32:55.048
They're playing the same game
you're playing but you're

00:32:55.048 --> 00:32:56.950
hearing them play games.

00:32:56.950 --> 00:32:59.390
PROFESSOR: Wow.

00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:00.845
Wow.

00:33:00.845 --> 00:33:01.611
AUDIENCE: That's deep.

00:33:01.611 --> 00:33:06.030
PROFESSOR: I could
say two groups

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:07.280
eavesdropping same game.

00:33:10.940 --> 00:33:15.562
AUDIENCE: I just wanted to say
that I think the thing which

00:33:15.562 --> 00:33:19.496
is really hard if you're working
with people who like

00:33:19.496 --> 00:33:23.384
don't really want to do a
role-playing thing is building

00:33:23.384 --> 00:33:28.990
a level of emotional intensity
and the fear of violence.

00:33:28.990 --> 00:33:31.194
Which is the point
of the training.

00:33:31.194 --> 00:33:34.330
They want them to be able to
deal with that kind of

00:33:34.330 --> 00:33:37.520
emotional stress on your feet.

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:39.620
So I was just wondering.

00:33:39.620 --> 00:33:41.460
Because I don't have
any good ideas.

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:44.554
Can anyone think of something
which works out?

00:33:44.554 --> 00:33:45.891
AUDIENCE: Which could genuinely
create a situation

00:33:45.891 --> 00:33:48.186
where one could be afraid
of violence?

00:33:48.186 --> 00:33:51.138
AUDIENCE: Without actually
having to

00:33:51.138 --> 00:33:53.620
threaten another person.

00:33:53.620 --> 00:33:56.260
AUDIENCE: I don't think you're
going to be able to do that.

00:33:56.260 --> 00:34:02.750
AUDIENCE: Well, if one player's
role is actually not

00:34:02.750 --> 00:34:06.160
to understand but to provide the
experience for the other?

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:09.090
And they are hearing the
recording that's giving them

00:34:09.090 --> 00:34:12.250
cues as to what to do?

00:34:12.250 --> 00:34:13.679
And the other player's not
agreed to this, and one of

00:34:13.679 --> 00:34:17.952
their cues is like and now, grab
the other player by the

00:34:17.952 --> 00:34:20.949
shoulders and shake them
violently and say something.

00:34:20.949 --> 00:34:23.575
You could possibly create
a situation where

00:34:23.575 --> 00:34:26.284
you're like, whoa.

00:34:26.284 --> 00:34:27.449
AUDIENCE: The problem is you
have to be able to get

00:34:27.449 --> 00:34:30.097
somebody who can do that without
cracking up in the

00:34:30.097 --> 00:34:30.639
middle of it.

00:34:30.639 --> 00:34:33.790
Because people do that when
they're nervous and

00:34:33.790 --> 00:34:35.710
uncomfortable.

00:34:35.710 --> 00:34:37.170
So you'd pretty much be
reduced to finding

00:34:37.170 --> 00:34:39.060
professional actors.

00:34:39.060 --> 00:34:42.676
And that's a limited resource
that puts a limitation on how

00:34:42.676 --> 00:34:43.911
widespread your game is.

00:34:43.911 --> 00:34:45.887
PROFESSOR: Even people
who are [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:49.345 --> 00:34:52.556
AUDIENCE: I had a thought where
you have a patient to

00:34:52.556 --> 00:34:53.791
patient interaction.

00:34:53.791 --> 00:34:55.521
So you have two people in
different rooms on the phone

00:34:55.521 --> 00:34:59.460
with each other trying to
negotiate something.

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:01.264
I don't know what it would be.

00:35:01.264 --> 00:35:04.228
AUDIENCE: And some other people
in each of their rooms

00:35:04.228 --> 00:35:07.192
that are talking in their ear
and trying to distract them

00:35:07.192 --> 00:35:08.180
from the conversation.

00:35:08.180 --> 00:35:13.120
PROFESSOR: OK, so patient
talks to patient.

00:35:13.120 --> 00:35:15.096
AUDIENCE: Maybe it could be
something like they're trying

00:35:15.096 --> 00:35:16.578
to negotiate [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:20.530 --> 00:35:23.000
PROFESSOR: With voices
in-person?

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:33.258
There is a specific article on
schizophrenia that I uploaded

00:35:33.258 --> 00:35:34.719
in your resources.

00:35:34.719 --> 00:35:35.969
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:35:41.050 --> 00:35:43.972
Patrick first, then Michelle?

00:35:43.972 --> 00:35:46.200
AUDIENCE: So I was thinking
something along the lines of

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:46.540
Battleship?

00:35:46.540 --> 00:35:49.445
If you have a doctor player
and a patient player and

00:35:49.445 --> 00:35:53.860
they're both looking at their
own version of the same board.

00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:58.640
And if the patient wants to get
from one end to the other

00:35:58.640 --> 00:36:01.245
and the doctor has to allow
whether or not they can make

00:36:01.245 --> 00:36:01.920
their move.

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:04.970
But on the doctor's board they
have different obstacles.

00:36:04.970 --> 00:36:07.306
And so the patient will
say, I want to move

00:36:07.306 --> 00:36:07.540
from here to here.

00:36:07.540 --> 00:36:09.300
And the doctor says, I can't
let you do that.

00:36:09.300 --> 00:36:11.490
I can let you go from
here to here.

00:36:11.490 --> 00:36:13.590
It'd be a game about
compromise.

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:19.306
PROFESSOR: OK, so sort
of a doctor mediated

00:36:19.306 --> 00:36:20.556
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:36:27.810 --> 00:36:32.003
And the Battleship analogy is
good to help us remember where

00:36:32.003 --> 00:36:34.220
the idea came from.

00:36:34.220 --> 00:36:35.170
Michelle?

00:36:35.170 --> 00:36:38.030
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so just
something that Owen said about

00:36:38.030 --> 00:36:39.890
trying to simulate fear.

00:36:39.890 --> 00:36:43.060
I mean, if you can add time
pressure to any game then I

00:36:43.060 --> 00:36:48.615
think that strong of a stress
situation can almost be fear.

00:36:48.615 --> 00:36:49.895
It can get [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:36:49.895 --> 00:36:54.363
PROFESSOR: Because what you're
really going for is stress in

00:36:54.363 --> 00:36:55.000
making decisions.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:57.876
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
created stress without the

00:36:57.876 --> 00:36:59.126
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:37:04.280 --> 00:37:08.190
AUDIENCE: So, an idea of how
to take the doctor mediated

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:11.250
board movement into
the real world?

00:37:11.250 --> 00:37:13.900
We're talking about these phone
to phone conversations.

00:37:13.900 --> 00:37:19.340
You could have the patient in
a room but he's blindfolded.

00:37:19.340 --> 00:37:22.210
And he's trying to move through
the room towards an

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:23.320
exit in the room or something.

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:25.920
And the doctor has some limited
information about the

00:37:25.920 --> 00:37:29.700
room and has to help him
navigate through the room.

00:37:29.700 --> 00:37:32.740
And he'll say, I want
to move forward.

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:36.460
And he'll say, well I can't
really let you do that because

00:37:36.460 --> 00:37:38.910
there's a pit fill of spikes
in front of you.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:40.740
And you wouldn't necessarily
actually have a real pit full

00:37:40.740 --> 00:37:41.160
of spikes in front of them.

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:44.020
But that kind of thing where
you're trying to gather

00:37:44.020 --> 00:37:46.690
information and help
them through this.

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:50.540
And you could put time pressure
on it and say, well I

00:37:50.540 --> 00:37:54.205
have to make a decision now
because he wants to move now.

00:37:54.205 --> 00:37:57.165
But I might accidentally move
him into an obstacle and have

00:37:57.165 --> 00:37:59.750
to deal with that.

00:37:59.750 --> 00:38:03.030
PROFESSOR: You could have a
thing where there are some

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:09.060
things that the patient needs
to do that the patient can't

00:38:09.060 --> 00:38:11.407
talk about.

00:38:11.407 --> 00:38:15.074
And it might be something like
the patient has to move every

00:38:15.074 --> 00:38:15.808
10 minutes.

00:38:15.808 --> 00:38:16.297
And only the patient
knows this.

00:38:16.297 --> 00:38:17.770
The doctor doesn't.

00:38:17.770 --> 00:38:20.710
AUDIENCE: The patient has a fear
of moving to the left.

00:38:20.710 --> 00:38:22.315
PROFESSOR: Or something
like that.

00:38:22.315 --> 00:38:22.750
AUDIENCE: Things like that.

00:38:22.750 --> 00:38:23.620
PROFESSOR: Patient has
to move left, right.

00:38:23.620 --> 00:38:26.820
AUDIENCE: I think it also
emphasizes the thing they were

00:38:26.820 --> 00:38:28.850
talking about on Monday
in that you are

00:38:28.850 --> 00:38:32.370
both trying to not--

00:38:32.370 --> 00:38:35.840
if it escalates to the point
where violence happens--

00:38:35.840 --> 00:38:38.270
you're both trying to not let
yourself get hurt but you're

00:38:38.270 --> 00:38:40.790
also trying to not hurt them.

00:38:40.790 --> 00:38:43.810
And I think incorporating that
in some way might be cool.

00:38:43.810 --> 00:38:47.290
PROFESSOR: Definitely a lot
of these ideas are sort of

00:38:47.290 --> 00:38:51.010
mechanicing the patient's
limitations are really,

00:38:51.010 --> 00:38:52.250
really, cool.

00:38:52.250 --> 00:38:55.700
But again, remember the audience
is the residents.

00:38:55.700 --> 00:38:57.910
So understanding what
a patient is

00:38:57.910 --> 00:39:00.168
going through is useful.

00:39:00.168 --> 00:39:04.309
But also thinking of rules
that help a resident

00:39:04.309 --> 00:39:07.385
understand what they can do
is also really important.

00:39:07.385 --> 00:39:09.114
So don't just think about
throwing a ton of limitations

00:39:09.114 --> 00:39:12.986
on them without also given
them a release.

00:39:12.986 --> 00:39:14.922
Things that they can
actively do.

00:39:14.922 --> 00:39:16.858
Words that they can carry out.

00:39:16.858 --> 00:39:20.488
AUDIENCE: So, working off the
time pressure thing, I think

00:39:20.488 --> 00:39:23.700
that it might make sense for
these kinds of escalation,

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:28.470
negotiation type, situations
for the person who's

00:39:28.470 --> 00:39:32.600
controlling the patient to know
what their triggers are.

00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:37.390
And every time a bad thing is
said, they just reduce the

00:39:37.390 --> 00:39:40.555
amount of time that the doctor
has to deal with them.

00:39:40.555 --> 00:39:45.400
And they say that, they just
say minus 10 seconds.

00:39:45.400 --> 00:39:48.015
And they have a timer and it
ticks down to reach the

00:39:48.015 --> 00:39:50.600
conclusion of the doctor getting
them to where they

00:39:50.600 --> 00:39:53.166
want to be.

00:39:53.166 --> 00:39:56.060
And rather than like raising
their voice, or being

00:39:56.060 --> 00:40:00.229
physically in some way, they
just adjust a timer.

00:40:00.229 --> 00:40:03.652
And when it gets down
to zero it's over.

00:40:03.652 --> 00:40:05.608
PROFESSOR: I can see that.

00:40:05.608 --> 00:40:06.858
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:40:14.900 --> 00:40:16.720
AUDIENCE: I'd just like to say,
one way you could make

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:18.860
your players feel pressure is
to give them a really flat

00:40:18.860 --> 00:40:20.870
goal hierarchy.

00:40:20.870 --> 00:40:21.160
Right?

00:40:21.160 --> 00:40:24.250
So a good example is
Missile Command.

00:40:24.250 --> 00:40:25.930
Does everyone know that game?

00:40:25.930 --> 00:40:27.440
Missile Command is
just six cities.

00:40:27.440 --> 00:40:29.020
And there's missiles coming
in and you have to

00:40:29.020 --> 00:40:29.930
shoot them all down.

00:40:29.930 --> 00:40:32.460
And each city is equally
valuable.

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:37.920
So if you lose one you're in a
drastically reduced position.

00:40:37.920 --> 00:40:40.870
Whereas games that tend to have
a more structured goal

00:40:40.870 --> 00:40:44.570
hierarchy basically means
you can afford a loss.

00:40:44.570 --> 00:40:44.900
Right?

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:46.440
So like chess is
a good example.

00:40:46.440 --> 00:40:48.450
Where you can say, OK I can
afford to lose this.

00:40:48.450 --> 00:40:51.720
Whereas in Missile Command you
can't afford to lose anything.

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:56.832
So by really capping what the
player has, what they can

00:40:56.832 --> 00:41:00.340
afford to lose, and then making
sure that all of their

00:41:00.340 --> 00:41:03.260
goals have equal value, that
means that every loss and

00:41:03.260 --> 00:41:06.400
every failure is felt
much more strongly.

00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:11.250
And I felt like, Dion when you
were doing your role play,

00:41:11.250 --> 00:41:14.932
there was this sense, especially
coming from Caesar,

00:41:14.932 --> 00:41:19.010
that any one thing that goes
wrong is really bad.

00:41:19.010 --> 00:41:21.260
There's a really thin
line there.

00:41:21.260 --> 00:41:23.150
PROFESSOR: That's especially
applicable when talking about

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:25.020
the doctor's goals,
not necessarily

00:41:25.020 --> 00:41:26.430
the patient's goals.

00:41:26.430 --> 00:41:28.820
AUDIENCE: I mean, from the
doctors' position right?

00:41:28.820 --> 00:41:31.711
You can't really afford a
lot of mistakes in these

00:41:31.711 --> 00:41:32.961
situations.

00:41:37.612 --> 00:41:40.738
AUDIENCE: I'm kind of aghast,
but I was listening to what

00:41:40.738 --> 00:41:45.700
you were saying and I have a
thought on how to combine lots

00:41:45.700 --> 00:41:47.895
of these different ideas.

00:41:47.895 --> 00:41:50.800
I'm envisioning a game where
there'd be two types of

00:41:50.800 --> 00:41:52.320
players, like you were

00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:54.680
mentioning, doctors and patients.

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:58.180
And there'd be one doctor
and multiple patients.

00:41:58.180 --> 00:42:00.950
It would be a turn based game
where you go around and give

00:42:00.950 --> 00:42:01.950
each person a turn.

00:42:01.950 --> 00:42:04.500
The doctor would always be
in relationship to each

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:06.210
individual one.

00:42:06.210 --> 00:42:11.100
And limited vocabulary I thought
was important, taking

00:42:11.100 --> 00:42:12.660
that from a couple
of the ideas.

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:14.950
The patient and the doctor each
have their own set of

00:42:14.950 --> 00:42:16.970
vocabularies and the
clash of that would

00:42:16.970 --> 00:42:20.380
introduce mechanic there.

00:42:20.380 --> 00:42:25.375
Where the patient has their own
vocabulary describing to

00:42:25.375 --> 00:42:28.030
the doctor what they like,
what they don't like.

00:42:28.030 --> 00:42:29.240
They know what they like.

00:42:29.240 --> 00:42:37.510
You could extract it to describe
anything the doctor

00:42:37.510 --> 00:42:39.090
would have in their
vocabulary.

00:42:39.090 --> 00:42:43.072
So, as one example the doctor
could give the patient a card

00:42:43.072 --> 00:42:45.530
with a shape or some
item on it.

00:42:45.530 --> 00:42:48.920
And the item has attributes like
color, shape, something

00:42:48.920 --> 00:42:51.190
that somebody could
like or dislike.

00:42:51.190 --> 00:42:56.820
In their list they could look
at this and see one of these

00:42:56.820 --> 00:43:00.450
things clashes with something
in my list of dislikes.

00:43:00.450 --> 00:43:01.460
I don't like that card.

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:05.760
I'm going to escalate my anger,
my displeasure, with

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:07.170
the situation.

00:43:07.170 --> 00:43:10.480
And so, the doctor has a limited
amount of time to deal

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:12.270
with each patient.

00:43:12.270 --> 00:43:16.800
As the patient's escalation
increases, they have less and

00:43:16.800 --> 00:43:18.250
less time to be dealt with.

00:43:18.250 --> 00:43:21.460
After a certain point the doctor
loses that patient, so

00:43:21.460 --> 00:43:24.300
you have that loss brought in.

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:27.860
And then after all the patients
have been lost the

00:43:27.860 --> 00:43:29.050
game is over.

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:32.050
The way that it would be won
is that the doctor is

00:43:32.050 --> 00:43:34.180
discovering the profile
of each patient.

00:43:34.180 --> 00:43:39.043
Because they are trying to, like
in a normal setting or a

00:43:39.043 --> 00:43:42.150
real world setting, you're
trying to discover the profile

00:43:42.150 --> 00:43:46.620
that allows the doctor to deal
with the patient in the best

00:43:46.620 --> 00:43:49.140
possible way so that they
aren't violent.

00:43:53.600 --> 00:43:57.623
PROFESSOR: So that seems to
play off the patient and

00:43:57.623 --> 00:44:01.487
doctor have different
rules and triggers.

00:44:01.487 --> 00:44:04.481
You've got a situation where, in
what you're suggesting, you

00:44:04.481 --> 00:44:07.770
have one doctor and
multiple patients.

00:44:07.770 --> 00:44:08.970
Which is accurate.

00:44:08.970 --> 00:44:11.620
I'm not quite sure that they'd
be dealing with them

00:44:11.620 --> 00:44:13.310
simultaneously but it might
not matter for the

00:44:13.310 --> 00:44:14.560
purposes of the game.

00:44:20.280 --> 00:44:24.690
It might be like those improv
games where you manage to

00:44:24.690 --> 00:44:30.720
figure out before time
runs out what a

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:31.870
person's profile is.

00:44:31.870 --> 00:44:35.510
That could be the
win condition.

00:44:35.510 --> 00:44:37.450
So it's equal win
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:44:37.450 --> 00:44:40.649
and equal loss if you don't
actually get it incorporated

00:44:40.649 --> 00:44:44.416
with something like you give a
trigger and reduce the amount

00:44:44.416 --> 00:44:46.146
of time you have available, the
number of turns available.

00:44:46.146 --> 00:44:47.628
It doesn't have to
be real time.

00:44:54.544 --> 00:44:55.794
So you can use it with
multiple patients.

00:45:00.472 --> 00:45:05.310
So that doesn't even necessarily
have to deal with

00:45:05.310 --> 00:45:09.050
talking about actual psychiatric
ward situations.

00:45:09.050 --> 00:45:13.048
That could be something like
objects and colors.

00:45:13.048 --> 00:45:14.860
It's more of a guessing game.

00:45:14.860 --> 00:45:18.480
That might be too abstract,
possibly, because in the end

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:23.490
you want people to be able to
tie it back to the experience.

00:45:23.490 --> 00:45:26.350
To be able to make an easy
connection to what it's like

00:45:26.350 --> 00:45:27.770
to be a resident or what
it's going to be

00:45:27.770 --> 00:45:29.860
like to be a resident.

00:45:29.860 --> 00:45:36.310
So this is where the theme
needs to be pretty clear.

00:45:36.310 --> 00:45:37.350
But in [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:45:37.350 --> 00:45:39.556
talked, fear is not meaning.

00:45:39.556 --> 00:45:43.900
Meaning is also tied pretty
well with your mechanics.

00:45:43.900 --> 00:45:47.310
So that's just something
to keep in mind.

00:45:53.240 --> 00:45:55.470
OK?

